const faq = [
  { q: 'What fairy tales feature ravens?', a: 'Several well-known European fairy tales feature ravens prominently: "The Seven Ravens" (Die sieben Raben, Brothers Grimm) in which seven brothers are transformed into ravens and must be rescued by their sister; "The Raven" (Die Rabe, Brothers Grimm) in which a princess is transformed into a raven; and "The Three Ravens" / "The Twa Corbies" — Scottish and English ballads about ravens feasting on a slain knight, with the more sinister "Twa Corbies" (two crows/ravens) version. Other examples appear in Italian (Calvino\'s collections), Slavic, and Scandinavian traditions.' },
  { q: 'What do ravens symbolise in fairy tales?', a: 'In European fairy tales, ravens consistently appear in connection with transformation (enchanted humans taking raven form), dark magic (witches and sorcerers use ravens), and threshold experiences (ravens appear at moments of death, choice, or crossing into the otherworld). Ravens carry knowledge: in multiple tale types, a raven overheard speaking reveals a secret or provides a solution. This storytelling role reflects the raven\'s real behavioural conspicuousness and apparent intelligence, amplified through narrative tradition.' },
  { q: 'What is "The Seven Ravens" about?', a: 'In the Brothers Grimm tale "The Seven Ravens" (Die sieben Raben, KHM 25), a father accidentally curses his seven sons, transforming them into ravens. His daughter, born after the transformation, sets out to rescue her brothers. She must travel to the glass mountain, sacrifice a finger as a key, and retrieve a ring to break the enchantment. The tale is classified under ATU 451 (The Maiden Who Seeks Her Brothers) in the Aarne-Thompson-Uther folktale classification index — a type found across European and Middle Eastern traditions.' },
];
